WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.375
Scenario: A supplier asks for broad administrator access to

2
00:00:03.375 --> 00:00:06.750
your cloud tenant to troubleshoot an integration. The contract

3
00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:10.500
does not clearly describe data processing, logging or access termination.

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.875
Safest response: Option A: Complete supplier/security review, define data

5
00:00:13.875 --> 00:00:17.250
and access scope, use named time-bound least-privilege accounts, log

6
00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.625
activity and document offboarding. Supplier access should be constrained,

7
00:00:20.625 --> 00:00:24.375
attributable, monitored and governed by explicit contractual and technical controls.

8
00:00:24.375 --> 00:00:27.750
Option B is unsafe because Broad access before due

9
00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:31.125
diligence expands exposure and can be difficult to unwind.

10
00:00:31.125 --> 00:00:34.500
Option C is unsafe because Shared credentials reduce accountability

11
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.875
and create an insecure secret-distribution path. Option D is

12
00:00:37.875 --> 00:00:41.250
unsafe because A verbal promise without enforceable scope and

13
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.625
technical controls is insufficient. In practice: Delay access until

14
00:00:44.625 --> 00:00:48.000
procurement, security, privacy and system owners document the supplier’s

15
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.375
need, safeguards, data terms, account scope and revocation plan.

16
00:00:51.375 --> 00:00:54.750
Key takeaway: Third-party trust must be supported by evidence,

17
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.000
scoped access, monitoring and lifecycle controls.
